# Wi-Fi

The phone reaches your laptop over USB, but the internet (to download
the AI models, for example) goes over Wi-Fi. Nerves configures
networking with [VintageNet](https://hexdocs.pm/vintage_net);
`VintageNetWiFi`'s `quick_` functions cover the common cases (not
enterprise networks). The phone remembers the network after a reboot.

This follows the "Configure WiFi" notebook of the official
[Nerves Livebook](https://github.com/nerves-livebook/nerves_livebook).
If the firmware was built with the venue's network (see below), the
phone is already connected and you can skip to the last cell.

## Pick a network

First, the networks the phone can see. The first scan after boot can come
back empty; run it again if yours is missing.

```elixir
networks = VintageNetWiFi.quick_scan()

Kino.DataTable.new(networks,
  name: "Wi-Fi networks",
  keys: [:ssid, :signal_percent, :frequency, :flags]
)
```

Pick your network and type its password (leave it empty for an open
network):

```elixir
if networks == [] do
  raise "No networks found yet: run the scan above again."
end

ssid_input =
  Kino.Input.select("Network", Enum.map(networks, &{&1.ssid, &1.ssid}))
  |> Kino.render()

password_input = Kino.Input.password("Password")
```

## Connect

Then connect:

```elixir
VintageNetWiFi.quick_configure(Kino.Input.read(ssid_input), Kino.Input.read(password_input))
```

After a few seconds this should say `:internet`:

```elixir
VintageNet.get(["connection"])
```

## Building the firmware with the venue's network

A workshop organiser can build the firmware with the venue's Wi-Fi, so
every phone joins it on first boot:

```sh
FP3_WIFI_SSID="venue-network" FP3_WIFI_PASSPHRASE="secret" mix firmware
```
